- new experiment suggested;
- repeated-dead-end avoidance;
- false exclusion/negative transfer;
- cost saved for later researchers;
- calibration of uncertainty/UNKNOWN;
- effect on future Jump/operator proposals.

A failure can be highly valuable even when no immediate successor solution exists.

